it has been one year since the start of  online learning on this day in 2020  school buildings around the state  closed and students remotely logged  online to access class if you remember  the last day of school  friday the 13th last year a news now  reporter kristen drummond live in the  newsroom  on the impact to the clark county school  district and the adjustments made to  continue  education well brian the governor made  the decision to close school buildings  to try and slow the spread of cobit 19.  now many expected online learning to  only last a few weeks  not an entire year for the fifth largest  school district  it's such a trip it's like twilight zone  joshua curtis reflects on this day  one year ago i think when it first  started i was thinking yeah an extended  spring break  but the father of six children with  three at the time  in ccsd schools later realized the  reality of the situation  that includes adjusting to distance  education  we had one computer that's it and so  when they want us to do home learning  yeah i was thinking that we definitely  need some help with this the district  immediately transitioning to online  learning and making paper packets  available for students at food  distribution  sites who did not have access to wi-fi  or a device  all my teachers are signing stuff on  my google classroom i spoke with some  students a year ago this week outside  cram  middle school the principal unlocked the  doors to let kids pick up items  needed for assignments and i just shoved  everything in my backpack  all not realizing then it would be 12 to  13 months before stepping back  on campus it's just been a year of  constant change rebecca garcia is the  nevada parent teacher association  president  she says the closures highlighted the  district's equity issues and  lack of resources in schools to open  sooner we're one of the lowest funded  states in america  and so the ability to return kids to  classrooms was delayed in many cases  i did not expect it to go this long at  all but curtis welcomes the district's  effort to now transition students and  staff  back in buildings this year has been  challenging but there has been some  growth free kindergarten to third grade  students started hybrid learning this  month and the other grade levels will  soon follow the next few weeks  now state superintendent joan eber  posted a vote a video online  celebrating the distance education  response efforts over the last year  she says on january 5th of this year  nevada was the first state to confirm  every k-12 student had a device and  internet access  while participating either full-time or  part-time in distance education  reporting live in the newsroom kristen  drummond 8 news now
 

